				<article id="post-821" class="entry content-bg single-entry post-821 post type-post status-publish format-standard has-post-thumbnail hentry category-kids-rooms">
	<div class="entry-content-wrap">
		
<div class="entry-content single-content">
	
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">LEGO storage ideas usually start the day someone steps on a brick. One set turns into five, the pieces mix together, and suddenly there are bricks in every corner of the house. The goal isn&#8217;t a perfect sorting system. It&#8217;s a setup that makes building easy and cleanup quick enough that kids will actually do it.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This guide covers how much to sort for the size of your collection, the best storage for loose bricks, sets and finished builds, and ideas for play tables, display shelves and small spaces.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-01-kids-room.jpg" alt="LEGO storage ideas: kids room corner with a white drawer unit of bricks sorted by color, a play table with bins and a shelf of brick-built houses" class="wp-image-820" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-01-kids-room.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-01-kids-room-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-01-kids-room-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-01-kids-room-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<div class="wp-block-group dsh-summary has-background" style="border-radius:12px;border-left-color:#8FA689;border-left-width:5px;background-color:#EEF3EC;padding-top:20px;padding-right:24px;padding-bottom:20px;padding-left:24px"><div class="wp-block-group__inner-container is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-0d2d72cd wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Key Takeaways</strong></p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Quick answer:</strong> The best LEGO storage ideas grow with the collection: one bin or a play mat bag for a few sets, 6 to 8 bins sorted by piece type as it grows, and a drawer cabinet for a large collection.</li>


<li>Sort only as much as your collection needs; one bin is fine for a few sets.</li>



<li>Sorting by piece type is easier to build from than sorting by color.</li>



<li>Give kids a place to build and a shelf to display finished models.</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Time:</strong> 1–3 hours · <strong>Cost:</strong> $0–150 · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Easy</p>
</div></div>


