#D9B48A" opacity="0.8"/><rect x="984" y="439" width="46" height="36" rx="3" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#D8CBBB" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="992" y="451" width="30" height="12" rx="2" fill="#342E2A" opacity="0.8"/><rect x="1036" y="439" width="46" height="36" rx="3" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#D8CBBB" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="1044" y="451" width="30" height="12" rx="2" fill="#C85A32" opacity="0.8"/><rect x="880" y="482" width="46" height="36" rx="3" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#D8CBBB" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="888" y="494" width="30" height="12" rx="2" fill="#D9B48A" opacity="0.8"/><rect x="932" y="482" width="46" height="36" rx="3" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#D8CBBB" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="940" y="494" width="30" height="12" rx="2" fill="#342E2A" opacity="0.8"/><rect x="984" y="482" width="46" height="36" rx="3" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#D8CBBB" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="992" y="494" width="30" height="12" rx="2" fill="#C85A32" opacity="0.8"/><rect x="1036" y="482" width="46" height="36" rx="3" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#D8CBBB" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="1044" y="494" width="30" height="12" rx="2" fill="#ECCDA0" opacity="0.8"/><rect x="880" y="525" width="46" height="36" rx="3" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#D8CBBB" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="888" y="537" width="30" height="12" rx="2" fill="#342E2A" opacity="0.8"/><rect x="932" y="525" width="46" height="36" rx="3" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#D8CBBB" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="940" y="537" width="30" height="12" rx="2" fill="#C85A32" opacity="0.8"/><rect x="984" y="525" width="46" height="36" rx="3" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#D8CBBB" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="992" y="537" width="30" height="12" rx="2" fill="#ECCDA0" opacity="0.8"/><rect x="1036" y="525" width="46" height="36" rx="3" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#D8CBBB" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="1044" y="537" width="30" height="12" rx="2" fill="#8FA689" opacity="0.8"/><rect x="60" y="750" width="1080" height="96" rx="14" fill="#8FA689"/><text x="600" y="790" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="#FFFFFF" text-anchor="middle">Tip: sort by shape, not color</text><text x="600" y="822" font-size="17" fill="#FFFFFF" text-anchor="middle">Builders look for the right shape first, so piece type is easier to search than color as a collection grows.</text><text x="600" y="878" font-size="15" fill="#6E645C" text-anchor="middle">Based on sorting advice from the Brick Architect LEGO Storage Guide. Collection sizes are a rough guide.</text><text x="1140" y="124" font-size="20" fill="#C85A32" text-anchor="end">dailyspacehacks.com</text></svg><figcaption style="text-align:center;font-size:0.9em;color:#6E645C;">Start simple, and add more sorting only when finding pieces gets hard.</figcaption></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Sorting by color looks tidy, but it isn&#8217;t always the easiest to build from. The <a href="https://brickarchitect.com/guide/bricks/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Brick Architect LEGO Storage Guide</a> points out that young builders usually care more about finding the right shape than the right color, and suggests starting with simple categories by piece type, such as bricks, plates, tiles, curved pieces, slopes and special pieces. Color sorting still works well for little kids and for display, so choose what fits your family.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Quick Pick: LEGO Storage Ideas by Situation</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Your situation</th><th>Start with these ideas</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A few sets, young kids</td><td>One bin (#1), play mat bag (#2)</td></tr><tr><td>Collection keeps growing</td><td>Bins by piece type (#3), drawer cabinet (#4), picture labels (#5)</td></tr><tr><td>Kids rebuild sets often</td><td>Sets in bags with manuals (#6), manual files (#7)</td></tr><tr><td>Need a place to build</td><td>Play table with storage (#8), rolling cart (#10)</td></tr><tr><td>Finished builds everywhere</td><td>Display ledges (#11), dust-free cases (#12), rotation (#13)</td></tr><tr><td>Small room or apartment</td><td>Under-bed bins (#14), closet shelf (#15), vertical storage (#16)</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">16 LEGO Storage Ideas</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">1. Start with one big bin</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For a small collection, one large bin or tub is enough. Kids can dump it out, build, and scoop everything back in. There&#8217;s no point sorting a few hundred pieces into twenty drawers.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">2. Use a play mat that turns into a bag</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-05-play-mat-bag.jpg" alt="Round drawstring play mat covered in toy building bricks that pulls closed into a storage bag" class="wp-image-817" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-05-play-mat-bag.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-05-play-mat-bag-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-05-play-mat-bag-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-05-play-mat-bag-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A round drawstring play mat is one of the smartest LEGO storage ideas for young kids. Bricks get spread out on the mat to play, then you pull the cord and the whole thing closes into a bag. Cleanup takes seconds.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">3. Sort into bins by piece type</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">As the collection grows, move to six to eight bins: bricks, plates, tiles, slopes, wheels and special pieces. It&#8217;s enough structure to find what you need without turning cleanup into a chore.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">4. Upgrade to a drawer cabinet</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-02-sorting-drawers.jpg" alt="Clear multi-drawer storage cabinet with toy building bricks sorted into small drawers with picture labels" class="wp-image-819" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-02-sorting-drawers.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-02-sorting-drawers-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-02-sorting-drawers-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-02-sorting-drawers-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For a big collection, a clear multi-drawer cabinet keeps everything visible. Sort by type first, then split the biggest categories by color in larger drawers. Clear fronts mean kids can see what&#8217;s inside before they open anything.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">5. Add picture labels</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Kids who can&#8217;t read yet can still put pieces away if each drawer or bin has a simple picture of the piece that belongs there. Labels are one of the easiest LEGO organization ideas to add, and they make cleanup faster for everyone.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Organize LEGO Sets and Instructions</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-06-sets-and-manuals.jpg" alt="Building brick sets stored in zip bags with instruction booklets in magazine files and labeled clear boxes on a shelf" class="wp-image-815" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-06-sets-and-manuals.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-06-sets-and-manuals-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-06-sets-and-manuals-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-06-sets-and-manuals-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">6. Keep favorite sets together in zip bags</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If your kids like to rebuild the same sets, store each set in its own zip bag with its instruction booklet. Line the bags up in labeled clear boxes by theme or size.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">7. File instruction booklets upright</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Magazine files keep booklets flat, upright and easy to flip through. Many instruction booklets can also be found online, so you may not need to keep every paper copy.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">LEGO Table With Storage and Build Zones</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">8. Set up a play table with bins underneath</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-03-play-table.jpg" alt="LEGO table with storage: low play table with a building baseplate top, fabric bins underneath and two small stools" class="wp-image-818" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-03-play-table.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-03-play-table-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-03-play-table-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-03-play-table-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A low table with a building plate on top and bins underneath gives kids a dedicated spot to build, and keeps half-finished projects off the floor. That&#8217;s why a LEGO table with storage is such a popular choice for families. Look for bins kids can pull out and put back on their own.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">9. Keep one baseplate for works in progress</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A spare baseplate works like a tray: kids can lift an unfinished build and set it on a shelf, so the table is free for the next project.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">10. Use a rolling cart</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A three-tier rolling cart holds bins of pieces and rolls to wherever kids want to build, then back into a corner or closet.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">LEGO Display Ideas</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-04-display-shelf.jpg" alt="LEGO display ideas: picture ledge shelf showing brick-built models of a house, car, plane and lighthouse above a dresser" class="wp-image-816" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-04-display-shelf.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-04-display-shelf-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-04-display-shelf-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-04-display-shelf-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">11. Show off builds on picture ledges</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Narrow picture ledges are perfect for finished models. They take almost no space and turn builds into decor. If you want to hang your own, our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/diy-floating-shelves/">DIY floating shelves guide</a> shows how to install shelves securely.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">12. Protect special builds from dust</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Clear display cases or boxes keep favorite models dust-free and safe from little siblings.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">13. Rotate what&#8217;s on display</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Give each builder a set amount of display space. When it&#8217;s full, the oldest build gets taken apart and the bricks go back into storage for new ideas.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">LEGO Storage Ideas for Small Spaces</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">14. Slide bins under the bed</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Low rolling bins under the bed hold a surprising number of bricks and keep them out of sight in a small bedroom.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">15. Use a closet shelf</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A shelf in the closet can hold sets, bins and the play mat bag. Our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-closet-organization-ideas/">small closet organization ideas</a> include tips for kids&#8217; closets, like keeping bins at their height.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">16. Go vertical</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A tall, narrow drawer tower or wall shelf uses height instead of floor space. If you have unused space under the stairs, our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/under-stairs-storage-ideas/">under stairs storage ideas</a> show how to turn it into a small play nook.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Organize LEGO in 5 Steps</h2>



<ol class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Gather everything.</strong> Collect bricks from every room, bag and box in one place.</li>



<li><strong>Pull out sets to keep together.</strong> Bag up sets your kids like to rebuild, with their instructions.</li>



<li><strong>Sort the rest simply.</strong> One bin for small collections, piece-type bins as it grows.</li>



<li><strong>Choose a build zone.</strong> A table, a mat or a corner where building happens.</li>



<li><strong>Make cleanup easy.</strong> Label everything and keep bins where kids can reach them.</li>
</ol>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-07-before-after.jpg" alt="LEGO storage before and after: bricks scattered across a kids room floor next to the same room organized with drawers and bins" class="wp-image-814" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-07-before-after.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-07-before-after-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-07-before-after-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/lego-storage-07-before-after-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The before-and-after above is the same room. The difference is a home for every brick, a place to build and a shelf for finished models. That&#8217;s the heart of all good LEGO storage ideas.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Common LEGO Storage Mistakes</h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Over-sorting a small collection.</strong> Kids won&#8217;t keep up with 30 drawers for a few sets.</li>



<li><strong>Bins that are too high.</strong> If kids can&#8217;t reach storage, they won&#8217;t use it.</li>



<li><strong>No place for works in progress.</strong> Unfinished builds end up on the floor.</li>



<li><strong>Mixing every set together.</strong> Keep favorite sets bagged if kids like to rebuild them.</li>



<li><strong>No display limit.</strong> Finished builds slowly take over every shelf.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">LEGO Storage FAQ</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">What is the best way to store a lot of LEGO?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A drawer cabinet or a set of bins sorted by piece type works best for large collections, with the biggest categories split by color. Clear drawers make pieces easier to find.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Should you sort LEGO by color?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For young kids, color sorting is simple and looks tidy. For bigger collections, sorting by piece type is usually easier to build from, because builders look for a shape first.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How do you store built LEGO sets?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Display favorites on narrow shelves or picture ledges, protect special builds in clear cases, and take older builds apart when display space runs out.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How do you store LEGO in a small space?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Use a play mat bag, under-bed bins, a closet shelf or a tall narrow drawer tower. Vertical storage saves the most floor space.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Bricks are only one part of the toy pile. Our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/toy-storage-ideas/">toy storage ideas</a> cover a simple toy rotation system, stuffed animals and keeping toys tidy in the living room.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Final Thoughts</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The best LEGO storage ideas grow with the collection: one bin at first, bins by type as it grows, and drawers for a big collection, plus a place to build and a shelf to show off finished models. Keep it simple enough that kids can clean up on their own, and the bricks will stay off the floor.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Save this guide to Pinterest before the next round of birthday and holiday sets arrives.</p>



